# Project Rules

## Project
- Laravel backend for a local-business directory (starting with cafes). It is API-only: no Blade views.
- The frontend is a separate Next.js app that consumes this API.
- The admin panel is built with Filament.
- Users can use the public API as guests. Authentication, when used, is OTP-based (no passwords).
- Solo project. Keep solutions simple and maintainable; avoid over-engineering.

## Coding standards
- Follow SOLID principles and Laravel conventions. Write code as a senior Laravel developer would.
- Keep controllers thin. Put validation in Form Requests, business logic in Services/Actions, and API output in API Resources.
- Use Eloquent relationships and eager loading. Avoid N+1 queries.
- Use strict types, type hints, and return types everywhere.
- Controllers live directly in App\Http\Controllers (no Api sub-namespace).
- Keep existing descriptive method names (getItems, getSingleItem, ...). Do not rename them to RESTful names unless asked.
- Identify categories in URLs by slug, never by numeric id.
- Column names are kept as constants in dedicated Fields classes (e.g. ItemInformationFields). Do not put form-building logic there.

## Filament
- Keep resource classes thin. Split each form section into its own schema class (e.g. ItemDetailsForm, LocationForm) and compose them in ItemForm.

## Docker (the project runs in containers)
- Never run php, composer, artisan, or npm directly on the host.
- Run commands inside the containers, for example:
  - docker compose exec app php artisan <command>
  - docker compose exec app composer <command>
- Services: app, webserver (nginx), vite, mysql, redis, queue.
- Never run destructive commands (migrate:fresh, db:wipe, docker compose down -v, rm -rf) without asking me first.

## Safety
- Never read, print, or edit .env or any file containing secrets.
- Do not install new packages without asking first and explaining why.
- Do not run git commit or git push. I do that myself.

## Workflow
- Before changing code, read the related files first. Do not guess names, methods, or table columns.
- If a request is ambiguous, ask one short clarifying question before writing code.
- Make small, focused changes. After each change, give a short summary of what changed and why.
- If you are unsure a package or Laravel feature exists, say so instead of inventing it.
- Reply in the same language I write in (Persian or English). Keep code, comments, and identifiers in English.